{
 "lead": "Every neuron adds up its inputs into a weighted sum z, then passes z through an activation function f. That function adds the bend that makes depth useful, and its derivative decides how much gradient flows back during training. This chapter gives the formula, range and derivative of sigmoid, tanh, ReLU and leaky ReLU, then softmax for the output layer, and says which one belongs where. Getting this choice right is half of making a network train at all.",
 "prereq": "You need the multilayer perceptron and the argument for non-linear activations from <a href=\"#multilayer-perceptrons-and-universal-approximation\">1.6</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Why a bend is needed, and sigmoid",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A unit adds up its inputs, z = Σᵢ wᵢxᵢ + b, where xᵢ are the inputs, wᵢ their weights and b the bias. Passing z straight on gains nothing: a weighted sum of weighted sums is still one weighted sum. The activation function f inserts a bend, a = f(z), and that bend is what makes a stack of layers stronger than one layer (see <a href=\"#multilayer-perceptrons-and-universal-approximation\">1.6</a>)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "f must be non-linear, and it must have a usable derivative, because backpropagation sends error backwards multiplied by f′(z): the derivative decides how much gradient each unit passes on (see <a href=\"#forward-propagation-and-backpropagation\">1.9</a>)."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e⁻ᶻ)",
     "where": "z is the weighted sum w·x + b; e ≈ 2.718 is Euler's number; σ(z) is the unit's output."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Sigmoid, also called the logistic function, squeezes any real number into (0, 1). Its derivative is σ′(z) = σ(z)(1 − σ(z)), at most 0.25, reached at z = 0. At z = 10 the output is 0.99995 and the derivative about 0.00005: the curve saturates at both ends, so a deep stack of sigmoid units passes almost no gradient. Outputs are never negative, so the unit is not zero-centred, and the weight updates of a unit tend to push the same way, which slows learning."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Sigmoid survives in the output layer of a yes/no classifier, where a number in (0, 1) reads as a probability (see <a href=\"#loss-functions-and-output-layers\">1.8</a>)."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "tanh and the ReLU family",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "tanh(z) = (eᶻ − e⁻ᶻ) / (eᶻ + e⁻ᶻ) = 2σ(2z) − 1",
     "where": "z is the weighted sum; the output lies in (−1, 1); the derivative is 1 − tanh²(z), at most 1 at z = 0."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "tanh is sigmoid stretched and shifted. It is zero-centred, which suits hidden layers better than sigmoid, but it still saturates at both ends, so very deep tanh stacks learn slowly too."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "f(z) = max(0, z)",
     "where": "z is the weighted sum; the output lies in [0, ∞); the derivative is 1 for z > 0 and 0 for z < 0, and is taken as 0 at z = 0 by convention."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "ReLU is zero for negative z and the identity for positive z. It costs one comparison, and on the positive side it never saturates, so a gradient of exactly 1 flows back through an active unit. That is why deep networks train at all. The catch is the dying unit: a ReLU whose z is negative for every training example outputs 0 and receives gradient 0, so its weights never move again."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "f(z) = z if z > 0, and αz if z ≤ 0",
     "where": "α is a small positive slope, α > 0, typically 0.01 or 0.1 (α = 0.1 is used in the figure and the example); the derivative is 1 for z > 0 and α for z < 0."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Leaky ReLU keeps a small slope below zero, so a unit still gets a gradient when z < 0 and a dead unit can recover. ReLU is the default for hidden layers of feed-forward and convolutional networks; leaky ReLU is a cheap safety net."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Softmax for the whole output vector",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "pᵢ = e^zᵢ / Σⱼ e^zⱼ",
     "where": "z = (z₁, …, z_K) are the K class scores from the last layer, zᵢ is the score of class i, and pᵢ is the predicted probability of class i."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Each pᵢ lies in (0, 1) and the pᵢ sum to 1, so the outputs read as the model's belief over K classes. Softmax belongs in the output layer of a single-label classifier, paired with cross-entropy loss (see <a href=\"#loss-functions-and-output-layers\">1.8</a>)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Softmax is not elementwise: every pᵢ depends on all the scores, so it cannot be applied unit by unit, and it has no place in a hidden layer. Subtracting the largest score from every zᵢ leaves the pᵢ unchanged but avoids overflow in e^zᵢ. The ranking survives: the largest score gets the largest probability."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Where",
      "Function",
      "Why"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Hidden layers, default",
       "ReLU",
       "cheap, gradient 1 when active"
      ],
      [
       "Hidden layers, negative values matter",
       "tanh",
       "zero-centred, bounded"
      ],
      [
       "Hidden layers, dying units seen",
       "leaky ReLU",
       "small negative slope keeps gradient"
      ],
      [
       "Output, yes/no",
       "sigmoid",
       "one probability in (0, 1)"
      ],
      [
       "Output, K classes",
       "softmax",
       "K probabilities summing to 1"
      ],
      [
       "Output, a number",
       "none (linear)",
       "loss needs the raw value"
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
