 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "State the range and the derivative of the sigmoid function.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "Range (0, 1); derivative σ′(z) = σ(z)(1 − σ(z)), maximum 0.25 at z = 0."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does a stack of many sigmoid layers learn slowly compared with ReLU layers?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Sigmoid saturates, and its derivative is at most 0.25. Backpropagation multiplies many such small factors, so the gradient reaching the early layers shrinks sharply. ReLU has derivative exactly 1 for z > 0, so the product does not shrink on the active side."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A leaky ReLU has α = 0.1. Give its output and derivative at z = −3.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "Output 0.1 × (−3) = −0.3; derivative α = 0.1."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "Find the softmax probabilities of the scores z = (0, 0, 0).",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Each e⁰ = 1, sum = 3, so p = (1/3, 1/3, 1/3) ≈ (0.333, 0.333, 0.333). Equal scores give equal probabilities."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "A hidden ReLU unit has negative z for every training example. Explain what happens to its gradient and weights, and how leaky ReLU changes the outcome.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "Its output is 0 and its derivative is 0, so the gradient through it is 0 for every example and its weights never change: the unit is dead. Leaky ReLU gives derivative α (a small positive number, α = 0.1 here) on the negative side, so a small non-zero gradient still reaches the weights and the unit can recover."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Why is softmax unsuitable for a hidden layer?",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "Softmax couples all units, so one unit's output depends on every other unit's score; it also forces the outputs to sum to 1, creating competition between hidden units that is not wanted. Hidden layers need a per-unit non-linearity, applied to each z separately."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why is softmax computed after subtracting the maximum score?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Adding the same constant to every zᵢ cancels in the ratio e^zᵢ/Σⱼ e^zⱼ, so the probabilities are unchanged. Subtracting the maximum keeps the largest exponent at 0, which prevents overflow when the scores are large."
  }
